A||||10||ArchMagi||87-88 4cyl Belt Tensioner Question||||1||
Z||000000||ArchMagi||12-19-2001||08:05 PM||||Is the pully and the tensioner one piece? or is the pully seperate?<P>Cause i can order the pully for 30$ CND but the parts guy was unsure if the auctual tensioner was included.<P>TIA<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.island.net/~lowell/archmagi.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||GTDude||12-19-2001||08:14 PM||||It is absolutely one unit.  What you have is a an idler.  Sorry.<P>phil pettus<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a_4_1.JPG"> <BR><P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||12-19-2001||09:24 PM||||My tensioner is about to throw the belt right off on my 87.  The pulley is so jacked out to the side that you can almost see the entire belt width right from the top, where as if it was lined up properly the belt would all be on the same plane.<P>So, I bought a brand new, GM belt tensioner AND pulley.  But, I am going to swap in a V6 now, and I never installed the unit.<P>It covers the whole space where the water pump would go if the engine was in a different car, and also is a part of the alternator bracket.  $30 CN for just the pulley sounds right, this is the whole unit.  I will sell it to you for $35 US plus shipping.  Email me if you are interested.<P>c311t@yahoo.com||||reg||1
Z||000003||Blacktree||12-20-2001||01:36 AM||||My daily driver is an 87 SE with the 2.5 4-cyl. When I replaced the timing gears last year, I had to remove the belt tensioner to get at the timing cover.<P>On my car the idler pulley and the idler arm are 2 seperate pieces. The pulley bolts onto the arm using a 15mm bolt... or maybe it was 13mm. I can't remember.<P>Whether or not the parts are SOLD seperately or as a single unit, I don't know.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.tweaktech.com" TARGET=_blank>Tweaktech</A><p>[This message has been edited by Blacktree (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||ArchMagi||12-20-2001||02:01 AM||||Yeah, thats what im trying to figure out, if there sold seperately.<P>Joe, i just might take you up on that offer, watch your mail.<P>Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ArchMagi||12-20-2001||02:02 AM||||double post<p>[This message has been edited by ArchMagi (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-20-2001||12:44 PM||||The tensioner unit is sold only as a whole assembly, with pulley.<P>You may be able to get the pulley separately in the aftermarket but you can't buy the tensioner from GM w/o the pulley... Rodney even caries just the pulley bearing if I remember.<P>In most cases the whole tensioner needs to be replaced. The pivot in the arm wears out and allows the arm to angle the pulley off track.<P>there are notes in my cave about replacing the tensioner.<P>The good thing is that a new tensioner will probably outlast the car.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||12-20-2001||01:13 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>In most cases the whole tensioner needs to be replaced. The pivot in the arm wears out and allows the arm to angle the pulley off track.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE></B><P>Yes, exactly the symptoms my 2.5 is experiencing, along with a nice "bell-like" ringing sound every once and a while.  The pulley is so crooked that it's hitting something and ringing like a bell.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B><BR>The good thing is that a new tensioner will probably outlast the car.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I forgot to mention that!  Archmagi, the one I have is the redesigned one, and is built better than the original one.  You should never have the problem again if you replace it.  I can get pics of it if you want.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||12-22-2001||03:52 PM||||Go ahead and replace the whole thing. Just did mine a couple months ago. Took a couple of hours of "not in a hurry" work. <BR>Drain your coolant first. The back of the tensioner covers a hole in the block that would normally be where a water pump is located. <P>If you've ever thought about removing the black fiber insulation from your shock towers, before you start this job would be an excellent time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000009||JohnF||12-22-2001||06:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>The good thing is that a new tensioner will probably outlast the car.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>OGRE!!!  Come on, now.  After buying my car new in 1988, I had to replace my pulley assy last year at about 111,000 miles.  <P>(sarcasm-on)Are you saying my new one will outlast my car????(/sarcasm-off)<P>Seriously, Ogre, you are right.  The pulley is one of our most neglected pieces that does one of our most important jobs.  It should be checked regularly. <P> <P>||||reg||10
Z||000010||theogre||12-22-2001||07:19 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> figure that the old one lasted 100,000 plus miles on most cars. Mine was replaced around 120,000.<P>The new ones are better made.... how many domestic cars/motors last beyond 200,000? You'll likely need the motor rebuilt at the very least well before the new tensioner quits.||||reg||12
